<!-- SECTION 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Tangent Ratio Calculator?</h2>
  <p>A tangent ratio calculator answers one question: for an acute angle in a right triangle, how does the side across from the angle compare with the side next to it? Enter the opposite side and the adjacent side, and the tool divides the opposite side by the adjacent side, returning <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=a/b]]')} />. A right triangle has an opposite side of 3 cm and an adjacent side of 4 cm, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4=0.75]]')} />. That 0.75 is the tangent ratio: it tells you the opposite side is 75 percent as long as the adjacent side. It is not an angle, so never read it as <span set:html={rich('[[0.75^\\circ]]')} />. The hypotenuse never enters this calculation; the opposite side and the adjacent side are sufficient on their own. Tangent is one of the fundamental trigonometric ratios, and its particular strength is comparing vertical rise with horizontal run, which is why it appears in roof pitch, road grades, and ramp slopes. The result is a dimensionless number: a pure comparison of two lengths, not a measurement in centimetres or degrees. If the angle in degrees is what you need, inverse tangent converts the ratio into degrees.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: Understanding the right triangle -->
<section id="sec-triangle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Opposite Side and Adjacent Side</h2>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has one angle of exactly <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, the angle between two perpendicular lines, like a wall meeting a level floor. The hypotenuse lies opposite that right angle and is always the longest side, because it must span the full diagonal across the corner. The other two angles share the remaining 90 degrees, so both are acute. The two legs meet at the right angle, perpendicular to each other, and it is their relationship with the hypotenuse that makes right-triangle trigonometry possible.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Opposite Side?</h3>
  <p>The opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the side directly across from the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, the one side that never touches its vertex. In practical problems it often plays the role of a vertical rise, an elevation difference, or a height: the wall height a ladder reaches, the rise of a roof, or the vertical component of a crane boom. Which side counts as opposite depends on the chosen reference angle, so switching from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to angle <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> swaps the opposite and adjacent labels. It appears in the numerator of the tangent formula because tangent measures how large that across-the-angle side is relative to the side next to the angle.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Adjacent Side?</h3>
  <p>The adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the leg that touches the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> without being the hypotenuse. In practical problems it often plays the role of a horizontal run or ground distance: the base of a ladder from a wall, the horizontal run of a roof, or the shadow length in a sun-angle problem. It is never the hypotenuse, a point worth checking every time, because the hypotenuse is the side facing the right angle. It appears in the denominator of the tangent formula, which is why tangent compares the opposite side against this leg rather than against the longest side.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the side opposite the right angle: in any diagram it faces the small square marking the <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} /> corner, and it is strictly longer than either leg. In practical problems it often plays the role of a sloping length: a ladder, a roof rafter, a guy wire, or a diagonal brace. It is not needed for the primary tangent calculation, because the tangent formula compares the two legs directly. If it is required for another problem, the Pythagorean theorem calculates it from the two legs: <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]]')} />.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('tangent-anatomy.svg')} alt="Right triangle with angle A bottom-left, gold opposite side a, blue adjacent side b, tan(A)=a/b" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Angle A sits at the bottom-left; tangent uses the gold opposite side a and the blue adjacent side b, not the hypotenuse c.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Tangent uses the two legs and does not require the hypotenuse. Keep this side-label convention consistent: <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> adjacent to angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> the hypotenuse opposite the right angle at <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: What is tangent -->
<section id="sec-whatis" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Tangent Ratio</h2>
  <p>The tangent of an acute angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is the opposite side divided by the adjacent side:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=\\frac{a}{b}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)]]')} /> means the tangent of angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the opposite side, and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the adjacent side. When the triangle is positioned with its base along the horizontal direction, tangent compares the vertical leg with the horizontal leg: rise over run in its purest form.</p>

  <h3>Understanding SOH-CAH-TOA</h3>
  <p>Students remember the three primary ratios with a single memory aid:</p>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li><strong>SOH:</strong> Sine = Opposite / Hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>CAH:</strong> Cosine = Adjacent / Hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>TOA:</strong> Tangent = Opposite / Adjacent.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>TOA corresponds to the tangent formula <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=a/b]]')} />: the <strong>T</strong> stands for tangent, the <strong>O</strong> puts the opposite side on top, and the <strong>A</strong> puts the adjacent side below. The aid helps you pick the correct numerator and denominator before any numbers enter the calculation. This section stays focused on tangent; the other two ratios have their own pages.</p>

  <h3>Why Tangent Does Not Use the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Tangent compares the two perpendicular legs directly, so the hypotenuse is unnecessary. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{3}{4}=0.75]]')} /></p>
  <p>The ratio already captures everything about how the opposite side compares with the adjacent side; bringing in the hypotenuse would change the question being asked. If the hypotenuse is needed for a different problem, the Pythagorean theorem calculates it from the legs. Tangent itself never asks for it.</p>

  <h3>Why Tangent Is a Dimensionless Ratio</h3>
  <p>Dividing one length by another cancels the physical units. With compatible units:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{3\\,\\text{m}}{4\\,\\text{m}}=0.75]]')} /></p>
  <p>The metres cancel, leaving a plain number. The same ratio comes from 300 cm and 400 cm, because the unit change affects both sides equally. The one requirement is that both measurements use compatible units: mixing metres with centimetres produces a meaningless number, while converting first keeps the ratio honest.</p>

  <h3>What Does a Tangent Value of 0.75 Mean?</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=0.75]]')} /> means the opposite side is 0.75 times the adjacent side, or 75 units of vertical rise for every 100 units of horizontal run in the same straight-line geometry. Picture a roof that climbs 75 cm for every 100 cm of horizontal run: that is exactly what 0.75 describes. It does not mean the angle is <span set:html={rich('[[0.75^\\circ]]')} />; the ratio and the angle are different quantities, and confusing them is one of the most common errors in trigonometry.</p>

  <h3>What Does a Tangent Value of 1 Mean?</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=1]]')} /> means <span set:html={rich('[[a=b]]')} />: the opposite and adjacent sides are equal. An isosceles right triangle has two equal legs, and because the two acute angles must share 90 degrees equally, each is <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A=45^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>Equal legs, equal acute angles, tangent exactly 1. This is the balance point of the tangent scale: below 1 the triangle is wider than it is tall, above 1 it is taller than it is wide.</p>

  <h3>Why Tangent Can Be Greater Than 1</h3>
  <p>Unlike sine and cosine, tangent has no upper limit for acute angles. If the opposite side outgrows the adjacent side, the ratio passes 1 without any problem. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=3]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{6}{3}=2]]')} /></p>
  <p>The opposite side is twice as long as the adjacent side. A tangent value greater than 1 is mathematically valid and does not indicate a calculator error; it simply describes a steep triangle, like a tall ladder standing close to a wall.</p>

  <h3>How Tangent Relates to Sine and Cosine</h3>
  <p>Start with the other two ratios:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=\\frac{b}{c}]]')} /></p>
  <p>Divide sine by cosine and the hypotenuse cancels:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{\\sin(A)}{\\cos(A)}=\\frac{a/c}{b/c}=\\frac{a}{b}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Therefore:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=\\frac{\\sin(A)}{\\cos(A)}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>This identity agrees with the opposite-over-adjacent definition, and it gives a useful cross-check: any tangent result should equal the sine divided by the cosine for the same angle.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('tangent-formula.svg')} alt="Three stages: 3-4-5 triangle, tangent formula tan(A)=a/b, result 0.75, opposite is 75 percent of adjacent" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Three stages: label the 3-4-5 triangle, write tan(A) = a/b, then divide 3 by 4 to get 0.75.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: Deriving the formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Tangent Formula Works</h2>
  <p>Start with the definition:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{\\text{Opposite}}{\\text{Adjacent}}]]')} /></p>
  <p>Then use this page's notation:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=\\frac{a}{b}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The opposite side belongs in the numerator because tangent measures how large the side across from the angle is; the adjacent side belongs in the denominator because it is the side being compared against. Swap them and you get cotangent, a different ratio entirely.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: A 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given an opposite side of <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, an adjacent side of <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, and a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula. <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=a/b]]')} /></p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the known values. <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4]]')} /></p>
  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Divide. <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.75}]]')} /></p>
  <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Interpret the result. The opposite side is 75 percent as long as the adjacent side.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Verify the triangle. <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />, which is <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]]')} />. The side lengths are consistent with a genuine right triangle.</p>

  <h3>Find the Angle From the Ratio</h3>
  <p>If the angle itself is required, apply inverse tangent to the ratio:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.75)]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{A\\approx36.87^\\circ}]]')} /></p>
  <p>Inverse tangent is also called arctangent. Calculating the tangent ratio and calculating the angle are separate operations: the ratio describes the triangle's shape, while inverse tangent converts that shape into degrees. The tangent ratio calculator on this page returns the ratio; recovering the angle is a follow-up step on a scientific calculator.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Tangent Ratio Calculator</h2>
  <p>The walkthrough below matches the live interface on this page. Each step matters, because a tangent result is only as trustworthy as the two measurements behind it.</p>
  <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
    <li><strong>Identify the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />.</strong> Tangent always works from one acute angle; naming it first fixes which side is opposite and which is adjacent.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, directly across from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />.</strong> This is the side that never touches the angle's vertex.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, which touches angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and is not the hypotenuse.</strong> Double-check that you have not grabbed the slanted side by mistake.</li>
    <li><strong>Obtain or measure both side lengths.</strong> A tape measure, a scale drawing, or a known specification all work, as long as the numbers describe the same triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the opposite-side length in the first input field.</strong> This is the numerator of the fraction.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the adjacent-side length in the second input field.</strong> This is the denominator.</li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible measurement units using the available unit selectors.</strong> Both inputs must be in the same unit, or converted to one before dividing.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm that both measurements are positive.</strong> A zero or negative side cannot belong to a real triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate tan(A).</strong> The tool divides the opposite side by the adjacent side.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the resulting tangent ratio.</strong> The answer is a plain number, with no degrees and no length units attached.</li>
    <li><strong>Check whether the result is less than, equal to, or greater than 1.</strong> Below 1 means the triangle is wider than it is tall; exactly 1 means equal legs; above 1 means the opposite side is longer.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset when you want to clear the inputs and begin another calculation.</strong> Fresh inputs deserve a fresh start, not leftover numbers from the previous problem.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator does not require the hypotenuse or an angle input. It performs one operation, the division <span set:html={rich('[[a/b]]')} />, and it performs it on exactly the two legs you identify.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6: Foundational examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Calculating Tangent</h2>
  <p>Four worked examples build the core skill before the practical applications. Each one follows the same rhythm: write the formula, substitute, divide, interpret.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: A 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{3}{4}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.75}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The opposite side is 75 percent as long as the adjacent side. Because the opposite side is the shorter leg here, the ratio sits below 1, describing a triangle wider than it is tall.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: A 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given an opposite side of <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, an adjacent side of <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{5}{12}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)\\approx0.4167}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The opposite side is approximately 41.67 percent as long as the adjacent side. The 5-12-13 triple is another Pythagorean triple, so <span set:html={rich('[[5^2+12^2=13^2]]')} /> confirms a genuine right triangle.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: A 45-45-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given an opposite side of <span set:html={rich('[[a=1\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, an adjacent side of <span set:html={rich('[[b=1\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, and a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(45^\\circ)=\\frac{1}{1}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(45^\\circ)=1}]]')} /></p>
  <p>Equal legs produce a tangent ratio of 1. This is the natural midpoint of the tangent scale and a value worth memorising: whenever the two legs match, the acute angles are 45 degrees each.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: A 30-60-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>For reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A=60^\\circ]]')} />, use an opposite side of <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, an adjacent side of <span set:html={rich('[[b=1\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />, and a hypotenuse of <span set:html={rich('[[c=2\\,\\text{cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(60^\\circ)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(60^\\circ)\\approx1.7321}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The opposite side is longer than the adjacent side, so the ratio exceeds 1, as it should for a 60-degree angle. Compare with the complementary angle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(30^\\circ)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\approx0.5774]]')} /></p>
  <p>Complementary acute angles have reciprocal tangent values, because swapping the reference angle swaps the opposite and adjacent labels. <span set:html={rich('[[0.5774 \\times 1.7321 \\approx 1]]')} />, which confirms the reciprocal relationship.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 7: Practical applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Examples of Using the Tangent Ratio</h2>
  <p>Tangent earns its place in the working world because so many real problems are really rise-over-run problems in disguise. Twelve worked examples show the pattern repeating across ramps, roofs, ladders, and surveying lines.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Calculating a Ramp's Slope</h3>
  <p>A simplified ramp rises 0.6 m over a horizontal run of 8 m. The vertical rise is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=0.6\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the horizontal run is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{0.6}{8}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.075}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The percentage grade is <span set:html={rich('[[0.075 \\times 100\\%=7.5\\%]]')} />, and the angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.075)\\approx4.29^\\circ]]')} />. The ramp rises 7.5 units vertically for every 100 units horizontally. The calculation does not establish accessibility or building-code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Calculating Roof Pitch</h3>
  <p>A roof rises 3 m over a horizontal run of 4 m. The vertical rise is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the horizontal run is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{3}{4}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.75}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The roof rises 0.75 units for each horizontal unit, a 75 percent slope, and the pitch angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.75)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Roof pitch is also expressed as rise over run in traditional units, such as inches of rise per 12 inches of run, which is the same ratio in different clothing. This ratio does not constitute a complete roof-design assessment.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Finding the Angle of a Ladder</h3>
  <p>A ladder reaches 4 m up a wall, and its base is 3 m horizontally from the wall. The angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is between the ladder and the ground. The vertical height is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=4\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the ground distance is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=3\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{4}{3}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(4/3)\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />. The vertical height is greater than the horizontal distance, which is why the ratio exceeds 1. The example explains geometry and does not establish whether the ladder is safely positioned.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: Estimating the Angle of Elevation to a Tree</h3>
  <p>A tree is 12 m tall, and its shadow is 9 m long on level ground. Assume the sun's rays create a simplified right-triangle model. The tree height is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=12\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the shadow length is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=9\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{12}{9}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The height is approximately 1.3333 times the shadow length, and the corresponding geometric angle is about <span set:html={rich('[[53.13^\\circ]]')} />. The example assumes level ground, a vertical tree, and a straight-line shadow model.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: Surveying an Elevation Difference</h3>
  <p>A surveying measurement shows a vertical elevation difference of 15 m over a horizontal distance of 20 m. The elevation difference is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=15\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the horizontal distance is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=20\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{15}{20}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.75}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The elevation difference is 0.75 times the horizontal separation, and the corresponding angle is about <span set:html={rich('[[36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Actual surveying can require instrument-height adjustments, corrections, and additional measurements beyond this simplified model.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Calculating a Road's Percentage Grade</h3>
  <p>A simplified road section rises 1.2 m over a horizontal distance of 24 m. The vertical rise is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=1.2\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the horizontal run is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=24\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{1.2}{24}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.05}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The percentage grade is <span set:html={rich('[[0.05 \\times 100\\%=5\\%]]')} />, and the angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.05)\\approx2.86^\\circ]]')} />. A 5 percent grade does not mean a 5-degree angle; this distinction is a common source of confusion, and the two numbers should never be used interchangeably.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Calculating a Staircase's Steepness</h3>
  <p>A simplified staircase has a total vertical rise of 2.4 m and a horizontal run of 3.2 m. The vertical rise is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=2.4\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the horizontal run is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=3.2\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{2.4}{3.2}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.75}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical rise is 75 percent of the horizontal run, and the angle is about <span set:html={rich('[[36.87^\\circ]]')} />. The calculation does not verify stair dimensions, comfort, or building-code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: Understanding a Guy Wire's Angle</h3>
  <p>A guy wire attaches to a pole 12 m above the ground, while its ground anchor is 5 m horizontally from the pole. The attachment height is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=12\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the ground distance is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=5\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{12}{5}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=2.4}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The angle is <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(2.4)\\approx67.38^\\circ]]')} />. The vertical height is 2.4 times the horizontal anchor distance, a steep wire by any measure. The tangent ratio does not determine wire tension, anchor strength, or structural safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 13: Calculating a Crane Boom's Direction</h3>
  <p>A simplified crane boom has a vertical component of 8 m and a horizontal projection of 6 m. The vertical component is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=8\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the horizontal projection is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=6\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{8}{6}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The corresponding angle is about <span set:html={rich('[[53.13^\\circ]]')} />. The vertical component is approximately 1.3333 times the horizontal projection. This is a simplified geometric example and does not establish crane capacity or operating safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 14: Estimating a Hiking Trail's Gradient</h3>
  <p>A simplified straight hiking section has a vertical elevation gain of 120 m and a horizontal distance of 800 m. The elevation gain is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=120\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the horizontal distance is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=800\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{120}{800}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.15}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The percentage grade is 15 percent, and the corresponding angle is about <span set:html={rich('[[8.53^\\circ]]')} />. This simplified model represents a constant straight-line gradient, not an entire winding hiking trail.</p>

  <h3>Example 15: Evaluating a Mechanical Brace</h3>
  <p>A diagonal mechanical brace has a vertical component of 1.5 m and a horizontal component of 2 m. The vertical component is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=1.5\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the horizontal component is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=2\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{1.5}{2}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.75}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical component is 75 percent of the horizontal component. The ratio does not determine the brace's strength, load capacity, or suitability.</p>

  <h3>Example 16: Finding the Angle of Elevation to a Building</h3>
  <p>An observer is standing 24 m horizontally from a building. The top of the building is 18 m above the observer's eye level. The vertical difference is the opposite side, <span set:html={rich('[[a=18\\,\\text{m}]]')} />, and the horizontal distance is the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[b=24\\,\\text{m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=\\frac{18}{24}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=0.75}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The angle of elevation is about <span set:html={rich('[[36.87^\\circ]]')} /> under the simplified geometry. This is the angle to the top relative to the observer's horizontal eye level, not necessarily the total building height.</p>

  <p>Tangent is a dimensionless ratio, but its practical value comes from comparing vertical rise with horizontal run, describing the steepness of a straight incline, calculating angles of elevation, and solving for missing sides when the angle is known. Three related quantities are easy to confuse, so keep them straight: the <strong>tangent ratio</strong> is vertical rise divided by horizontal run; the <strong>percentage grade</strong> is the tangent ratio multiplied by 100 percent; the <strong>angle</strong> is the result of applying inverse tangent to the ratio. Tangent alone never determines structural safety, load capacity, accessibility, or engineering compliance.</p>

