<!-- SECTION 8: Angle relationship -->
<section id="sec-angle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Relationship Between Tangent and Angle Size</h2>
  <p>Tangent increases as the reference angle increases, and the geometry explains why. When angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is small, the opposite side is short relative to the adjacent side, so the ratio sits near 0. As the angle widens, the opposite side becomes a longer share of the adjacent side, and the ratio climbs: past 1 at <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />, then without bound as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />. The table below uses illustrative side lengths; the ratio, not the absolute size of the triangle, determines the angle.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /></th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /></th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /></th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">Tangent ratio</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[30^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{3}]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 0.5774</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5">1</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[60^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{3}]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 1.7321</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4"><span set:html={rich('[[75^\\circ]]')} /></td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Approximately 3.7321</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 3.7321</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The pattern is simple: tangent is small when the opposite side is short relative to the adjacent side, equals 1 at <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />, exceeds 1 for larger acute angles, approaches zero as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ]]')} />, and grows without bound as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />. Triangles with the same angle have the same tangent ratio even when their side lengths differ, because the ratio captures shape, not size.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('tangent-angle-change.svg')} alt="Three triangles at 30, 45, 60 degrees with tangent values 0.5774, 1, 1.7321; tangent rises with angle" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">As angle A grows, the opposite side becomes a longer share of the adjacent side, so tan(A) rises.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 9: Finding the opposite side -->
<section id="sec-side" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find the Opposite Side Using Tangent</h2>
  <p>The tangent formula can be rearranged to find the opposite side when the adjacent side and angle are known. Start with <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=a/b]]')} /> and multiply both sides by <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=b\\tan(A)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The opposite side equals the adjacent side multiplied by the tangent of the angle. Given an adjacent side of <span set:html={rich('[[b=10\\,\\text{m}]]')} /> and an angle of <span set:html={rich('[[A=30^\\circ]]')} />, and knowing <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(30^\\circ)=1/\\sqrt{3}\\approx0.5774]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[a=10\\times0.5774]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{a\\approx5.774\\,\\text{m}}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The vertical rise is approximately 5.774 m for a 10 m horizontal run at this angle.</p>
  <p>The adjacent side can be found the same way by rearranging again:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=\\frac{a}{\\tan(A)}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With an opposite side of <span set:html={rich('[[a=6\\,\\text{m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A=45^\\circ]]')} />: since <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(45^\\circ)=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=6/1]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{b=6\\,\\text{m}}]]')} />. These are mathematical rearrangements of the same formula. The calculator on this page returns the tangent ratio from side lengths; it does not directly calculate missing sides.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 10: Finding an angle -->
<section id="sec-recover" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find an Angle From a Tangent Ratio</h2>
  <p>The tangent ratio and the angle are different quantities: one is a dimensionless comparison of sides, the other is measured in degrees. When the ratio is known and the angle is required, inverse tangent can be used:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A=\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Inverse tangent is also called arctangent. Given <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=0.75]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.75)]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{A\\approx36.87^\\circ}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The angle is approximately <span set:html={rich('[[36.87^\\circ]]')} /> when the calculator is set to degrees.</p>
  <h3>Why the Calculator's Angle Mode Matters</h3>
  <p>Angles can be measured in degrees or radians, and a scientific calculator must be set to the intended mode before inverse tangent is applied. A full circle is 360 degrees or <span set:html={rich('[[2\\pi]]')} /> radians, so <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ=\\pi/4]]')} /> radians. The tangent ratio itself has neither degree nor radian units; those units apply to the angle recovered from it. The tangent ratio calculator on this page returns the ratio directly and does not return an angle.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 11: Common mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Tangent</h2>
  <p>Most tangent errors come from a flipped fraction, a mislabeled side, or confusing the ratio with the angle. Run through this list before trusting a result.</p>
  <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
    <li><strong>Reversing the formula and calculating <span set:html={rich('[[b/a]]')} /> instead of <span set:html={rich('[[a/b]]')} />.</strong> This returns cotangent, not tangent. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, the reversed fraction gives 1.3333; the correct one gives 0.75.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing tangent with sine.</strong> Tangent divides the opposite side by the adjacent side; sine divides the opposite side by the hypotenuse. If the hypotenuse is in the denominator, the answer is sine, not tangent.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing tangent with cosine.</strong> Cosine divides the adjacent side by the hypotenuse. Tangent never uses the hypotenuse at all, so a hypotenuse in either position signals the wrong ratio.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing tangent with cotangent.</strong> Cotangent is the reciprocal, adjacent over opposite. The similar names cause real errors, but the fraction order is exactly reversed.</li>
    <li><strong>Including the hypotenuse in the primary tangent formula.</strong> The hypotenuse plays no role in <span set:html={rich('[[a/b]]')} />. If it appears in the calculation, the wrong formula was used.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing up the opposite and adjacent sides.</strong> Both are legs, so the labels are easy to swap. Always trace from the reference angle: across from it is opposite, touching it is adjacent.</li>
    <li><strong>Identifying the sides relative to the wrong reference angle.</strong> Switching from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to angle <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> swaps the opposite and adjacent labels, which flips the ratio to its reciprocal.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming tangent must be less than 1.</strong> That rule belongs to sine and cosine. Tangent has no upper limit for acute angles, and a result above 1 is perfectly valid.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting the tangent ratio as an angle in degrees.</strong> A result of 0.75 is a ratio, not <span set:html={rich('[[0.75^\\circ]]')} />. Treating it as degrees feeds nonsense into any follow-up calculation.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting the tangent ratio with measurement units.</strong> The units cancel in the division, so "0.75 m" is wrong. Write the plain number and keep the units in the input measurements where they belong.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering zero or negative side lengths.</strong> A triangle cannot have a zero or negative side, so these inputs signal a data entry error or a missing measurement, not a valid edge case.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing incompatible measurement units.</strong> An opposite side of 3 m with an adjacent side of 40 cm gives 7.5, a meaningless number. Convert to a single unit first: 3 m and 4 m give the correct 0.75.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding too early.</strong> Rounding an intermediate division to two decimals and then continuing the calculation compounds the error. Keep full precision through every step and round only the final answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing percentage grade with angle in degrees.</strong> A 5 percent grade comes from a 0.05 tangent, but the angle is <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan^{-1}(0.05)\\approx2.86^\\circ]]')} />, not 5 degrees. Grade and angle are different quantities.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming the tangent ratio determines the absolute size of a triangle.</strong> Tangent describes shape, not size: infinitely many triangles share a tangent of 0.75, from a 3 cm opposite side to a 3 km one. The ratio fixes the proportions; actual lengths need at least one side measurement.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct: A Worked Comparison</h3>
  <p>Given an opposite side of <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and an adjacent side of <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />:</p>
  <p><strong>Incorrect:</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[4/3\\approx1.3333]]')} />. This is the cotangent ratio for angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, not the tangent ratio. The numerator and denominator are reversed.</p>
  <p><strong>Correct:</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4=0.75]]')} />.</p>
  <p><strong>Verify using sine and cosine:</strong> with <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=3/5=0.6]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=4/5=0.8]]')} />. Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)/\\cos(A)=0.6/0.8=0.75]]')} />, which confirms the tangent calculation.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 12: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Tangent Result Is Valid</h2>
  <p>A valid, non-degenerate right triangle requires two positive legs, <span set:html={rich('[[a \\gt 0]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b \\gt 0]]')} />, measured in compatible units, so the tangent result must be positive:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\tan(A)=\\frac{a}{b} \\gt 0}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>There is no requirement for the opposite side to be shorter than the adjacent side, so the result may sit below, at, or above 1. A result that is zero or negative usually indicates invalid inputs or reversed labels.</p>
  <h3>Method 1: Check the Ratio</h3>
  <p>Re-verify <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=a/b]]')} /> directly. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4=0.75]]')} />. Because the opposite side is shorter than the adjacent side, the ratio should land below 1; if a second hand calculation disagrees, a side was mislabeled or a unit was mixed.</p>
  <h3>Method 2: Check the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Calculate the hypotenuse when needed: <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{9+16}=5]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />. This confirms the dimensions of the 3-4-5 right triangle. The hypotenuse is a verification value here, not part of the primary tangent formula.</p>
  <h3>Method 3: Check Using Inverse Tangent</h3>
  <p>For a tangent ratio of 0.75: <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(0.75)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Because the ratio sits below 1, the angle should land below <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />; if it does not, the side labels are probably swapped. Small differences may arise from rounding.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 13: Choosing a ratio -->
<section id="sec-vs" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Tangent vs. Sine, Cosine, and Cotangent</h2>
  <p>Each ratio compares a different pair of sides, using the same reference angle and side labels:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Ratio</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Formula</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">What it compares</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Sine</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sin(A) = a/c</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side to hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cosine</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">cos(A) = b/c</td><td class="py-2.5">Adjacent side to hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Tangent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">tan(A) = a/b</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side to adjacent side</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cosecant</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">csc(A) = c/a</td><td class="py-2.5">Hypotenuse to opposite side</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Secant</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sec(A) = c/b</td><td class="py-2.5">Hypotenuse to adjacent side</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cotangent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">cot(A) = b/a</td><td class="py-2.5">Adjacent side to opposite side</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Tangent and cotangent are the reciprocal pair: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=1/\\tan(A)]]')} />. If <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=0.75]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333]]')} />. Reach for tangent when both legs are known or when a problem involves rise over run; reach for sine or cosine when the hypotenuse enters the work, and cotangent when the comparison runs the other way. Keep tangent central: the neighbouring ratios have their own pages with full treatments.</p>
  <p>For the neighbouring ratios, see the <a href="/sine-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Sine Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/cosine-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Cosine Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/cotangent-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Cotangent Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/secant-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Secant Ratio Calculator</a>, and the <a href="/cosecant-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Cosecant Ratio Calculator</a>.</p>
</section>

